[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Typing React Hooks with TypeScript: A Comprehensive Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"React Hooks, introduced in React 16.8, revolutionized how we manage state and side effects in functional components. When combined with TypeScript, Hooks become even more powerful, providing type safety and improved developer experience. This tutorial will guide you through typing various React Hooks with TypeScript, covering common scenarios and best practices."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Setting Up Your TypeScript React Project"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into typing Hooks, ensure you have a React project set up with TypeScript. You can use Create React App with the TypeScript template or configure TypeScript in an existing project."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"npx create-react-app my-ts-app --template typescript
cd my-ts-app"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"Typing the `useState` Hook"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `useState` Hook is fundamental for managing component state. TypeScript allows you to explicitly define the type of the state variable."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Usage"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState } from 'react';

interface MyComponentProps {
  initialCount?: number;
}

const MyComponent: React.FC<MyComponentProps> = ({ initialCount = 0 }) => {
  const [count, setCount] = useState<number>(initialCount);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `useState<number>(0)` explicitly tells TypeScript that `count` is a number. The initial value passed to `useState` helps TypeScript infer the type, but it's good practice to be explicit."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Typing with Union Types"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes, your state can hold multiple types. Use union types to represent this."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState } from 'react';

const MyComponent: React.FC = () => {
  const [message, setMessage] = useState<string | null>(null);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Message: {message || 'No message'}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setMessage('Hello, TypeScript!')}>Set Message</button>
      <button onClick={() => setMessage(null)}>Clear Message</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `message` can be either a string or `null`. This is useful for representing optional or uninitialized states."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Typing Complex Objects"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"For complex state objects, define an interface or type to represent the structure."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState } from 'react';

interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

const MyComponent: React.FC = () => {
  const [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null);

  return (
    <div>
      {user ? (
        <>
          <p>Name: {user.name}</p>
          <p>Email: {user.email}</p>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p>No user loaded</p>
      )}
      <button onClick={() => setUser({ id: 1, name: 'John Doe', email: 'john.doe@example.com' })}>Load User</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `User` interface defines the shape of the user object, ensuring type safety when accessing properties like `user.name` and `user.email`."},{"order":19,"type":"heading","text":"Typing the `useEffect` Hook"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `useEffect` Hook is used for performing side effects in functional components. TypeScript helps ensure that your side effects are handled correctly."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Usage"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const MyComponent: React.FC = () => {
  const [data, setData] = useState<string | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Simulate fetching data
    setTimeout(() => {
      setData('Data loaded!');
    }, 1000);

    // Optional cleanup function
    return () => {
      // Clean up resources here (e.g., clear timers, unsubscribe)
      console.log('Component unmounted or dependencies changed');
    };
  }, []); // Empty dependency array means this effect runs only once on mount

  return (
    <div>
      <p>{data || 'Loading...'}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `useEffect` Hook doesn't require explicit typing, but you should ensure that the types of any variables used within the effect are correctly defined (e.g., `data` in this example)."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Typing Event Handlers in `useEffect`"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"When adding event listeners within `useEffect`, ensure you correctly type the event handler."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useEffect } from 'react';

const MyComponent: React.FC = () => {
  useEffect(() => {
    const handleScroll = (event: Event) => {
      console.log('Scrolled!', event.currentTarget);
    };

    window.addEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);

    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Scroll down to see the effect!</p>
      <div style={{ height: '200vh' }}></div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `handleScroll` function is typed with `(event: Event)`, ensuring that the event object is correctly typed."},{"order":28,"type":"heading","text":"Typing the `useContext` Hook"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `useContext` Hook allows you to access context values in functional components. TypeScript helps ensure that the context value is correctly typed."},{"order":30,"type":"code","text":"import React, { createContext, useContext } from 'react';

interface ThemeContextType {
  theme: 'light' | 'dark';
  toggleTheme: () => void;
}

const ThemeContext = createContext<ThemeContextType | undefined>(undefined);

interface Props {
  children: React.ReactNode;
}

const ThemeProvider: React.FC<Props> = ({ children }) => {
  // Default theme is light
  const [theme, setTheme] = React.useState<'light' | 'dark'>('light');

  const toggleTheme = () => {
    setTheme(theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light');
  };

  const value: ThemeContextType = {
    theme,
    toggleTheme,
  };

  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value={value}>
      {children}
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
};

const useTheme = () => {
  const context = useContext(ThemeContext);
  if (!context) {
    throw new Error('useTheme must be used within a ThemeProvider');
  }
  return context;
};

const MyComponent: React.FC = () => {
  const { theme, toggleTheme } = useTheme();

  return (
    <div style={{ backgroundColor: theme === 'light' ? '#fff' : '#333', color: theme === 'light' ? '#000' : '#fff' }}>
      <p>Current theme: {theme}</p>
      <button onClick={toggleTheme}>Toggle Theme</button>
    </div>
  );
};

const App: React.FC = () => {
  return (
    <ThemeProvider>
      <MyComponent />
    </ThemeProvider>
  );
};

export default App;"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `ThemeContextType` defines the shape of the context value.  `createContext<ThemeContextType | undefined>(undefined)` ensures that TypeScript knows the context's structure. The `useTheme` custom hook provides a type-safe way to access the context."},{"order":32,"type":"heading","text":"Typing the `useReducer` Hook"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `useReducer` Hook is used for managing complex state logic. TypeScript helps ensure that your reducer and actions are correctly typed."},{"order":34,"type":"code","text":"import React, { useReducer } from 'react';

interface State {
  count: number;
}

type Action = { type: 'INCREMENT' } | { type: 'DECREMENT' };

const initialState: State = { count: 0 };

const reducer = (state: State, action: Action): State => {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'INCREMENT':
      return { count: state.count + 1 };
    case 'DECREMENT':
      return { count: state.count - 1 };
    default:
      return state;
  }
};

const MyComponent: React.FC = () => {
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {state.count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' })}>Increment</button>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'DECREMENT' })}>Decrement</button>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `State` defines the shape of the state object, and `Action` is a union type representing the possible actions. The `reducer` function is typed to accept a `State` and an `Action` and return a `State`.  This ensures that the reducer function handles actions correctly and returns the expected state."},{"order":36,"type":"heading","text":"Typing Custom Hooks"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"Custom Hooks allow you to extract reusable logic from components. TypeScript ensures that your custom Hooks are correctly typed and return the expected values."},{"order":38,"type":"code","text":"import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

interface UseFetchResult<T> {
  data: T | null;
  loading: boolean;
  error: string | null;
}

const useFetch = <T>(url: string): UseFetchResult<T> => {
  const [data, setData] = useState<T | null>(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState<boolean>(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const fetchData = async () => {
      try {
        const response = await fetch(url);
        if (!response.ok) {
          throw new Error(`HTTP error! Status: ${response.status}`);
        }
        const json = await response.json();
        setData(json);
      } catch (e: any) {
        setError(e.message);
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    };

    fetchData();
  }, [url]);

  return { data, loading, error };
};

export default useFetch;"},{"order":39,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `useFetch` Hook is a generic Hook that fetches data from a URL. `UseFetchResult<T>` defines the shape of the return value, and `<T>` allows you to specify the type of the data being fetched.  This provides type safety when using the Hook."},{"order":40,"type":"code","text":"import React from 'react';
import useFetch from './useFetch';

interface Post {
  userId: number;
  id: number;
  title: string;
  body: string;
}

const MyComponent: React.FC = () => {
  const { data: post, loading, error } = useFetch<Post>('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1');

  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;
  if (error) return <p>Error: {error}</p>;

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{post?.title}</h1>
      <p>{post?.body}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;"},{"order":41,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, we use the `useFetch` Hook to fetch a post from a JSONPlaceholder API. We specify the type of the data as `Post`, ensuring that the `post` variable is correctly typed."},{"order":42,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"Typing React Hooks with TypeScript enhances code quality, reduces errors, and improves the developer experience. By explicitly defining types for state, actions, and context values, you can leverage TypeScript's type checking capabilities to catch potential issues early in the development process. This tutorial covered common scenarios and best practices for typing `useState`, `useEffect`, `useContext`, `useReducer`, and custom Hooks. Embrace TypeScript to build robust and maintainable React applications."}]
